gbjf(){return"Music starts with your body: feel the steady beat, notice high and low, loud and soft, fast and slow. You don\u2019t read notes yet \u2014 you listen and move."},
gbjg(){return"Now notes get names and places on the staff. You learn how long each one lasts, how they fill a bar, and how to read a simple tune in C major."},
gbjh(){return"Both hands, both clefs. Sharps and flats give notes new colours; you measure the distance between notes (intervals) and stack them into your first chords."},
gbji(){return"Music gets richer: minor keys, the circle of fifths, chords with a special 7th, and how phrases come to rest (cadences). You start to hear WHY chords move."},
gbjj(){return"The advanced toolkit: chord inversions, transposing instruments, reading a full score, and the shapes and colours (form, modes) composers use to build whole pieces."},
